html:div.html:details:
Proof.
    We want to maximize

    \begin {align*}
\mathcal {L}(\theta ) &= \mathbb {E}_{x \sim  p_X, y \sim  p_Y}\Big [\log \sigma (f_{\theta }(x)) + \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(y)))\Big ] \\
&= \int  \log \sigma (f_{\theta }(x)) P_X(x) + \int  \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(y))) P_Y(y) \\
&= \int  \log \sigma (f_{\theta }(z)) P_X(z) + \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(z))) P_Y(z)
    \end {align*}

    Since we are maximizing f(s), we can just maximize the integrand i.e.

      \begin {align*}
        \frac {\mathrm {d}}{\mathrm {d}f(z)} \Big [\log \sigma (f_{\theta }(z)) P_X(z) + \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(z))) P_Y(z)\Big ] = 0
      \end {align*}

    Solving,

        \begin {align*}
          P_X(z)\big (1 - \sigma (f(z))\big ) - P_Y(z)\sigma (f(z)) = 0 &\iff  \sigma (f(z)) = \frac {P_X(z)}{P_X(z) + P_Y(z)} \\
          &\iff  f(z) = \log \left (\frac {P_X(z)}{P_Y(z)}\right )
        \end {align*}

fr:mainmatter.html:div:
Example. 
  Loop unrolling cannot make use of the large distance between dependencies for large k below. 
  for (i = k; i < n; i++)
    a[i] += a[i - k];
  For k = 3, Software pipelining can perform something like
  for (i = 3; i < n; i++)
    a[i - 2] += a[i - 2 - k];
    a[i - 1] += a[i - 1 - k];
    a[i] += a[i - k];
  You can imagine how this becomes more useful as k grows large.

Example. 
  There is an actual dependency between instructions here that cannot be circumvented by OoO hardware 
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    sum += a[i];
However, this can be re-written at the software level to get some parallelism within loop bodies as:
for (i = 0; i < n; i += 4) {
    s0 += a[i];   s1 += a[i+1];
    s2 += a[i+2]; s3 += a[i+3];
}
sum = (s0 + s1) + (s2 + s3);}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let \rho (s) \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}|}  be the initial state distribution. Then consider the following LP
    \begin {align*}
\begin {array}{ll} \displaystyle  \max _{V \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}|}} & \displaystyle  \sum _{s \in  \mathcal {S}} \rho (s) V(s) \\ \text {s.t.} & V(s) \leq  r(s,a) + \gamma  \sum _{s'} P(s' \mid  s,a) \, V(s'), \qquad  \forall  s,a. \end {array}
    \end {align*}
    Translating this into our standard form, we have

\mathbf {c} \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}|} with c_s = \rho (s)

A \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}| \times  |\mathcal {A}| \times  |\mathcal {S}|} with A_{(s,a),s'} = -\delta _{s,s'} + \gamma  P(s'|s,a).

    Substituting this in for the dual formulation gives us
    \begin {align*}
& \min _{y(s,a) \geq  0} & & \sum _{s,a} r(s,a) \, y(s,a) \\ & \text {s.t.} & & \sum _{a} y(s,a) - \gamma  \sum _{s',a'} P(s \mid  s',a') \, y(s',a') = \rho (s), \qquad  \forall  s
    \end {align*}
    Here \mathbf {y} can be seen as the discounted occupancy measure. This allows us to recover a randomized optimal policy via
    \mathbb {P}(\pi ^*(s) = a) = \frac {y(s, a)}{\sum _{a} y(s,a)}

If f is first-order continuously differentiable, then f is convex iff
f(\boldsymbol {y}) \geq  f(\boldsymbol {x}) + \langle  \nabla  f(\boldsymbol {x}), \boldsymbol {y} - \boldsymbol {x}
\rangle

fr:mainmatter.html:div:
Remark. Holds with equality if and only if
f is linear on \mathcal {X}
X is a constant almost surely.

html:div.html:details:
Proof.
Note that 

\begin {align*}
 D_{\mathrm {KL}}(P || Q) &= \mathbb {E}_P \left [\log \left (\frac {P(x)}{Q(x)}\right )\right ] \\
&= \mathbb {E}_P \left [-\log \left (\frac {Q(x)}{P(x)}\right )\right ] \\
\end {align*}

By Jensen's Inequality,

  \begin {align*}
    D_{\mathrm {KL}}(P || Q) &\geq  -\log  \left (\mathbb {E}_P \left [\frac {Q(x)}{P(x)}\right ]\right ) = 0
  \end {align*}

Proof.
Define p_i = \frac {a_i}{A}, q_i = \frac {b_i}{B}, then 

\begin {align*}
  D_{\mathrm {KL}}(p || q) &= \sum  \frac {a_i}{A} \log {\frac {B a_i}{A b_i}} \\
  &= \sum  \frac {a_i}{A} \log {\frac {a_i}{b_i}} - \sum  \frac {a_i}{A} \log {\frac {A}{B}} \\
  &= \sum  \frac {a_i}{A} \log {\frac {a_i}{b_i}} - \log {\frac {A}{B}} \\
\end {align*}

By Gibb's inequality,

\begin {align*}
  \sum  \frac {a_i}{A} \log {\frac {a_i}{b_i}} - \log {\frac {A}{B}} \geq  0
  &\implies 
  \sum  \frac {a_i}{A} \log {\frac {a_i}{b_i}} \geq  \log {\frac {A}{B}} \\
  &\iff 
  A \sum  a_i \log {\frac {a_i}{b_i}} \geq  A \log {\frac {A}{B}}
\end {align*}

as desired.

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
The optimal estimator g^* : \mathcal {Y} \to  \mathcal {X} is

\begin {align*}
  g^*(y) = \arg \max _{x \in  \mathcal {X}} P_{X | Y}(x | y)
\end {align*}

html:div.html:details:
Proof.
    Recall that

    \begin {align*}
      P_{e}(g) &= \sum _{x}\sum _y P_{XY}(x, y) \cdot  \mathbf {1}_{x \neq  g(y)} \\
      &= \sum _{y} P_Y(y) \sum _{x} P_{X|Y}(x|y) \mathbf {1}_{x \neq  g(y)} \\
      &= \sum _{y} P_Y(y) (1 - P_{X|Y}(g(y)|y)) \\
    \end {align*}

To minimize this, we take

  \begin {align*}
    g^*(y) = \arg \max _{x} P_{X|Y}(x | y)
  \end {align*}

as desired.

Proof.
Use chain rule to expand I(U; (V, W)) in two ways i.e. 

  \begin {align*}
    I(U; (V, W)) = I(U; V) + I(U; W | V) = I(U; W) + I(U; V | W)
  \end {align*}

Since U \to  V \to  W is a Markov chain, the conditional independence (U \perp  W) \mid  V makes I(U; W | V) = 0. Therefore,

\begin {align*}
  I(U; V)  &= I(U; W) + I(U; V | W) \\
&\geq  I(U; W)
\end {align*}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Suppose that X \iff  Y \iff  \hat {X} and let p_e = \mathbb {P}(X \neq  \hat {X}). Then, we have 

    \begin {align*}
      H(p_e) + p_e \log (|\mathcal {X}| - 1) \geq  H(X \mid  \hat {X}) \geq  H(X \mid  Y)
    \end {align*}

html:div.html:details:
Proof.
    By Chebyshev's Inequality,

    \begin {align*}
      \mathbb {P}(|\hat {X} - \mu | > a) \leq  -\frac {\sigma ^2}{a^2}
    \end {align*}

    Since X_i's are independent, 
\mathrm {Var}[\hat {X}] = \frac {1}{n^2} \sum _{i = 1}^{n} \mathrm {Var}[X_i]
    Therefore,

    \begin {align*}
      \lim _{n \to  \infty } \mathbb {P}(|\hat {X} - \mu | > a) \leq  \lim _{n \to  \infty }-\frac {\sigma ^2}{n a^2} \to  0
    \end {align*}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let \{X_{n}:n\geq 1\} denote i.i.d. \mathcal {X}-valued r.v. with distribution P_{X}. Then,


\begin {align*}
 \lim _{n\to \infty }-\frac {1}{n}\log  p_{X^{n}}(X_{1},\ldots ,X_{n}) &= \lim _{n \to  \infty } -\frac {1}{n} \log  \prod _{i = 1}^{n} P_X(X_i) \\
 &= \mathbb {E}_{X \sim  p_X}[-\log  p_X(X)] \\ 
 &= H(X)
\end {align*}

Equivalently, for large n

  \begin {align*}
    \left |-\frac {1}{n} \log  p_{X^n}(X^n) - H(X)\right | \leq  \epsilon 
  \end {align*}

with high probability.

Fix an \epsilon >0. Then, an \epsilon -typical set with respect to the pmf p_{X}, denoted by A_{\epsilon }^{(n)}, is defined as
\left \{X^n \in  \mathcal {X}^n \mid  \left |-\frac {1}{n} \log  p_{X^n}(X^n) - H(X)\right | \leq  \epsilon  \right \}
Alternatively,
A_{\epsilon ^{(n)}} = \left \{X^n \in  \mathcal {X}^n \mid  2^{-n(H(X) + \epsilon )} \leq  p_{X^n}(X^n) \leq  2^{-n(H(X) - \epsilon )}\right \}

A coding system with parameters (n, \theta ) consist of an encoder and a decoder, where an encoder is a function
e: \mathcal {X}^n \to \{1, 2, \ldots , \theta \}
and a decoder is a function f: \{1, 2, \ldots , \theta \} \to \mathcal {X}^n

html:details.html:p:
For the other direction, suppose we have some R achievable. By definition, for all \epsilon  > 0, for all sufficiently large n there exists a Coding system with parameters (n, \theta ) such that

  \begin {align*}
    \frac {\log  \theta }{n} < R + \epsilon , \quad  \mathbb {P}(\text {error}) < \epsilon 
  \end {align*}

Use Fano's Inequality to show this is not possible.

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
A randomized hypothesis test is a channel which takes in the data X and outputs a \{0,1\} valued random variable Z , where Z=i represents that the test declares that H_{i} is true, for i\in \{0,1\} .
X\sim P\longrightarrow \boxed {P_{Z|X}}\longrightarrow Z In some cases, it is sufficient to focus on non-randomized or deterministic tests, for which P_{\mathbb {Z}|X=x} is a delta function. That is, for a given realization of X , the output of the test is deterministic (it is equal to H_{0} or H_{1} with probability 1). Such deterministic tests can be equivalently represented by a function \Psi , or a subset B of the domain X :
\Psi (X)=\mathbf {1}_{B}(X)=\begin {cases}1,&\text {if}X\in B\\ 0,&\text {if}X\not \in B.\end {cases}

In any binary hypothesis testing problem, a test  P_{Z|X|}  can incur two types of errors:
\begin {align*}
   \pi _{1|0}(P_{Z|X})&=\mathbb {P}_{H_{0}}\ (Z=1) \quad  \text {(type-I error of false alarm rate)} \\
 \pi _{01}(P_{Z|X})&=\mathbb {P}_{H_{1}}\ (Z=0) \quad   \text {(type-II error or missed detection rate)}
.\end {align*}

Consider a hypothesis testing problem with observations  X^{n}\stackrel {\text {i.i.d.}}{\sim }P_{X} , with the null  H_{0} : P_{X} =P_{0}  and alternative  H_{1}:P_{X}=P_{1} . Then, the optimal NP test with level  \alpha \in  (0,1]  has the following structure:

 \Psi _{NP}^{*}(X^{n})=\begin {cases}1,&\text { if }L(X^{n})>\tau \\ \gamma ,&\text { if }L(X^{n})=\tau \\ 0,&\text { if }L(X^{n})<\tau .\end {cases} 

In the above display,  L(X^{n})  denotes the likelihood ratio  p_{1}(X^{n})/p_{0}(X^{n}) , and  \gamma \in [0,1]  and  \tau \geq 0  are constants that ensure  \pi _{1|0}(\Psi ^*_{NP})=\mathbb {P}_{H_{0}}(\Psi ^*_{NP}(X^{n})=1)=\alpha  . Namely, 
\tau  = \inf \{\tau  \mid  \alpha  \geq  \mathbb {P}(L(X^n) > \tau )\} and \gamma  = \frac {\alpha  - \mathbb {P}(L(X^n) > \tau )}{\mathbb {P}(L(X^n) = \tau )}

  To construct, a universal fixed rate code, we need a code that satisfies (i) and (ii) from Fixed rate universal code. 


Let  |\mathcal {X}|=m , and for any  n\geq 1  and a given  R>0 , define  R_n=R-m \frac {\log  n+1}{n}

Consider the subset A_n \subset  \mathcal {X}^n, defined as A_n = \{x^n \in  \mathcal {X}^n \mid  H(\hat {P}_{x^n} \leq  R_n)\}  

Then just define the encoder in the usual way i.e. for encoder e_n give every x^n \in  A_n a unique code and everything else map to the same code.

Then the decoder can easily identify everything in A_n and it suffices to show that in the limit the probabilities concentrate around A_n.

html:div.html:details:
Proof.
  We know that the optimal codeword for  P  has lengths approximately equal to  \ell _{P}(x)\approx \log (1/p(x)) , where  p  denotes the pmf of  P . Whereas the optimal codeword for  Q  assigns lengths  \ell _{Q}(x)\approx \log (1/q(x)) . Then, the redundancy is


\begin {align*}
 \mathcal {R}(Q,P)&=\mathbb {E}_{X\sim  Q}[\ell _{P}(X)-\ell _{Q}(X)]\approx \mathbb {E}_{X\sim  Q}[-\log (p(X))+\log (q(X))] \\
&=\mathbb {E}_{X\sim  Q}\left [\log \left (\frac {q(X)}{p(X)}\right )\right ]=D_{\mathrm {KL}}(Q\parallel  P)
.\end {align*}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let \mathcal {X} denote an alphabet of size m , and let \mathcal {P}=\{P_{1},\ldots ,P_{k}\} denote a collection of k distinct distributions on \mathcal {X} (every P_{i} can be identified with a pmf in \Delta _{m} ). Now, consider the following two-player game:
Player 1, representing adversary, chooses a distribution P from the set \mathcal {P}
Player 2, representing source coder, constructs a code according to the distribution Q \in \Delta _m without knowledge of P The goal of Player 2is to identify a Q that minimizes the worst-case redundancy {\mathcal {R}}^{*}\equiv {\mathcal {R}}^{*}({\mathcal {P}}):=\operatorname *{min}_{Q\in \Delta _{m}}\operatorname *{max}_{P\in {\mathcal {P}}}{\mathcal {R}}(P,Q)=\operatorname *{min}_{Q\in \Delta _{m}}\operatorname *{max}_{P\in {\mathcal {P}}}D_{\mathrm {KL}}(P\parallel Q).
